From 403fc076392085105dde2bb500c0862a796fd50d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Film Grain Workbench <noreply@4kvcd.invalid>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 05:42:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] --fga 4: the three film grain analyses without coding

For Film Grain Workbench, which adds VVenC's film grain parameters to a
VVC encode made elsewhere. Applies to the local VVenC master 066f71fb
(upstream master with the open 4KVCD pull requests, among them the film
grain analysis work; equal to the 4KVCD fork's master). --fga 4 runs the analyzers of --fga 1, 2 and 3 on
the same pictures, each with its own parameter sets, as each does on its
own: --fga 1 without the temporal residual, and without the analysis of
the picture after a shot start that restarts the shot's statistics in
--fga 2 and 3, so each mode keeps its own previously analysed picture
for the scene cuts and the POC its sets apply from. It codes nothing:
pictures take the first pass's skip path, and ALF and rate control are
off (both would wait for coded pictures; neither touches MCTF or the
analysis). The output holds, for every picture, an access unit
delimiter (EncLib waits for a non-empty access unit after each input
picture), and after each analysis, for each mode that analysed the
picture, a user data unregistered SEI (UUID
393cc818-0a4b-4cef-8e2d-f1605b9577c0; data: the mode, then the POC from
which the set applies, big-endian) followed by the FGC SEI message of
that parameter set. MCTF does only the analysis's work, on the pictures
the analysis looks at: motion estimation, the analysis's filter and the
temporal residual, not the encoding's filtering, nor the noise levels,
SCC detection and block importance that serve the coding. --fga 1 to 3
write the same streams as before.

--fgastacked 1 (with --fga 4 only): every input picture is twice the
height given, the source picture on top of the decoded encode the grain
is for. --fga 2's and 3's sets are then also fitted, picture by picture,
to the grain that encode lost, as a real encode fits its own
(xScaleToLostGrain, now on a state per mode): each fitted set follows a
marker whose mode byte is 128 + the mode and whose POC is the picture's
own. The spectra are computed on input over the source's size and kept
with the picture until its SEIs are written. Without --fgastacked,
--fga 4's sets are the analyzers' own (the fit needs a reconstruction).
Checked: the source over the decoded --fga 2 and 3 encodes of 96
pictures of a 1080p HDR film (QP 28, preset faster) gives fitted sets
byte-identical to those encodes' own, 96 of 96 in both modes; --fga 1
to 4 write byte-identical streams without --fgastacked.

Also: SEIuserDataUnregistered frees its data with delete[], as an array.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
